{
  "image_name": "dde30e79125a59c8bc724a0fa0e420ff.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two laser-based routes for generating vacuum-ultraviolet light to probe or excite trapped thorium-229 ions. In both cases, the goal is to convert infrared or visible light into a much higher-frequency VUV beam, shown in purple, and deliver it to the ions.\n\nOn the left, the scheme uses xenon gas and resonance-enhanced four-wave difference mixing. Two continuous-wave lasers are first stabilized and then amplified into pulses. One beam is frequency-tripled, producing two input frequencies that are combined in a xenon cell. An energy-level sketch indicates that the mixing is resonantly matched to xenon transitions, which helps generate the desired VUV frequency. The resulting VUV light is directed toward the thorium ions.\n\nOn the right, the VUV source is based on solid-state high-harmonic generation. A Ti:sapphire frequency-comb system, with repetition-rate and offset-frequency stabilization and an f–2f interferometer, feeds an infrared enhancement cavity inside a vacuum chamber. Within the cavity, the intense IR beam is focused onto an AlN-on-sapphire target, where a harmonic-conversion process generates the fifth harmonic. This produces the VUV output beam that is sent to the thorium ions.",
